## 1. Face-incidence identity

For each induced harmonic graph,

\[
L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}=2M_r-4R_r.
\]

Summing gives `2(e_C-e_BB+C_att)-4R_loc`. The canonical full face ledger gives
`e_C=2R_4+2f+q-k`. Therefore

\[
E_\Sigma
 =4(R_4-R_{loc})+4f+2(q-k-e_{BB}+C_{att})-2d_\Sigma,
\]

which is (AD.4). No global injectivity of local boundary occurrences is needed.

## 2. Endpoint-profile Type-II inequalities

The audited Type-II `c_B=0` theorem makes every loaded-loaded contact a force-hull
side. Pairwise interior-disjoint attachment cells use such a side at most once, so
`C_att≤e_BB`. The audited Section-126 localization gives
`R_4-R_loc=O(D^{2/3})`. On the Section-118 endpoint/common-cap profile the audited
diameter-moment bound is `d_Σ/D≥S_*−o(1)`. Since `f≤Q`, the exact ledger
`b+Q=2(q-k)`, and `b/D→π+δ`, the upper estimate

\[
E_\Sigma/D\le10t-4π-4δ-2S_*+o(1)
\]

has the correct signs. Section 122 supplies the lower bound `c_0`; taking liminf and
limsup yields (AD.8). No near-geodesic assumption is hidden in this step.

## 3. Orbit-envelope composition on that profile

The §171 feasibility expression is used exactly in its audited-working scope, with
`δ=b/D-π`; its denominator is bounded below by the stated `2√Δ_2+0.0004` feasibility
condition. Its `t` derivative is positive. On `0<δ≤0.00014`, the cubic derivative
term is bounded below by `1170`, while the negative denominator derivative is below
`4`; thus `∂_δF_{t_0}>1160` is conservative.

Using `t_0=1.8585`, the exact constants from the audited floors give
`A_0=0.0001357884958...` and `F_{t_0}(A_0)=-0.1350172...`. Therefore all
endpoint/common-cap `t≤t_0` limits are excluded: (AD.8) either has a negative right
side or places
`δ` in `[0,A_0]`, where monotonicity makes `F_t(δ)` no larger than the negative
endpoint value.

The decimal checks are reproduced by
`verify_target_c_aggregate_detour_endpoint_floor.py`; they are not used to assert
global optimality or to alter theorem state.

